---
import GuideLayout from "@layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const title = "Diminished Value vs. Total Loss | FairClaimCalculator";
const description =
  "A short decision guide: whether your vehicle was repaired or declared a total loss determines which claim and which of this site's tools applies to you.";
const path = "/guides/claim-process/diminished-value-vs-total-loss-which-claim/";
---
<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  h1="Diminished Value vs. Total Loss: Which Claim Do I Have?"
  cluster={{ name: "Claim Process", href: "/guides/claim-process/" }}
  publishedDate="2026-08-08"
  reviewedDate="2026-08-08"
  summary="One fact decides which path applies to you: was your vehicle repaired, or was it declared a total loss? This short guide routes you to the right tools and guides based on that single fact, and explains the handful of situations where both can apply."
  sources={[
    "General routing note: this guide organizes and cross-links this site's own existing tools and guides based on one factual distinction (repaired vs. total loss); it is not a citation to an external regulation or authority. See each linked calculator or guide for its own sourcing.",
  ]}
  relatedLinks={[
    { label: "Total-Loss Offer Audit calculator", href: "/calculators/total-loss-offer-audit/" },
    { label: "Diminished Value Baseline calculator", href: "/calculators/diminished-value-baseline/" },
    { label: "What Is Diminished Value?", href: "/guides/diminished-value/what-is-diminished-value/" },
    { label: "How Insurers Value a Total-Loss Vehicle", href: "/guides/total-loss/how-insurers-value-a-total-loss-vehicle/" },
    { label: "Check My Offer — answer two questions", href: "/check-my-offer/" },
  ]}
>
  <h2>The one fact that decides it</h2>
  <p>
    Every tool and guide on this site sorts into one of two paths, and the split comes down to a
    single fact about your vehicle: was it repaired, or was it declared a total loss?
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Repaired → possible diminished value.</strong> If your insurer or the at-fault
      driver's insurer paid to fix your vehicle, it may now be worth less on the market than an
      equivalent vehicle with no accident history — even with a flawless repair. That gap is
      diminished value. See <a href="/guides/diminished-value/what-is-diminished-value/">What Is
      Diminished Value?</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Declared a total loss → total-loss valuation and settlement.</strong> If the insurer
      decided repair cost (plus, in some states, a statutory percentage threshold) makes fixing the
      vehicle uneconomical, it's declared a total loss instead of repaired, and you're offered a
      cash settlement based on the vehicle's pre-loss value rather than a repair. See
      <a href="/guides/total-loss/how-insurers-value-a-total-loss-vehicle/">How Insurers Value a
      Total-Loss Vehicle</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="limits-note">
    <strong>These are mutually exclusive for the same damage event.</strong> A single vehicle,
    for a single accident, is either repaired or totaled — never both. The confusion usually comes
    from timing (see below), not from both applying at once.
  </div>

  <h2>Quick self-check</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you…</th><th>…start here</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>The insurer repaired (or is repairing) the vehicle, and you want to check whether it's now worth less</td>
        <td><a href="/calculators/diminished-value-baseline/">Diminished Value Baseline calculator</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Same as above, but the vehicle is leased rather than owned</td>
        <td><a href="/calculators/leased-vehicle-diminished-value/">Leased Vehicle Diminished Value calculator</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The insurer sent you a total-loss valuation report and a cash offer</td>
        <td><a href="/calculators/total-loss-offer-audit/">Total-Loss Offer Audit calculator</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>You have a total-loss settlement check and want to see how the deductions add up</td>
        <td><a href="/calculators/settlement-check-breakdown/">Settlement Check Breakdown calculator</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>You're deciding whether to keep your totaled vehicle instead of surrendering it</td>
        <td><a href="/calculators/salvage-value/">Salvage / Owner-Retained Value calculator</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>You're still not sure</td>
        <td><a href="/check-my-offer/">Check My Offer — answer two questions</a></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Where the two paths can seem to overlap</h2>
  <p>
    A few situations make it feel like both apply, even though only one actually does for a given
    vehicle and accident:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Multiple accidents on the same vehicle.</strong> A vehicle repaired after an earlier
      accident, then totaled in a later one, genuinely has both a past diminished-value question
      (from the first accident) and a current total-loss question (from the second) — they're just
      two separate claims, potentially years apart.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You're not sure yet which the insurer will decide.</strong> Early in a claim, before
      the insurer has chosen repair or total loss, neither tool has enough information yet. Once
      you have either a repair estimate or a total-loss valuation report in hand, the self-check
      table above applies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You disagree with the insurer's repair-vs-total-loss decision itself.</strong> That's
      a different question from either tool on this site — it's about the threshold decision, not
      the value calculation downstream of it. See
      <a href="/guides/total-loss/state-total-loss-threshold-laws/">State Total-Loss Threshold
      Laws</a> for how that threshold decision is typically made.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this guide cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    It cannot tell you which path your specific claim is on — that depends on your insurer's actual
    repair-or-total-loss decision, which this site has no way to know in advance. It also cannot
    tell you whether diminished value is recoverable in your state or what your total-loss offer
    should be; those are handled by the calculators and guides linked above, each with its own
    sources and limitations stated separately.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
